### When do you use an idempotency key?

Use it when a client command may be retried and you must return the same outcome, classically payments. The client generates a unique key and sends it on every attempt.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;

    public PaymentService(StringRedisTemplate redis) {
        this.redis = redis;
    }

    public String charge(String idempotencyKey, String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Atomic claim: SET NX ensures only first caller wins
        Boolean claimed = redis.opsForValue()
                .setIfAbsent("idem:" + idempotencyKey, "IN_PROGRESS", Duration.ofHours(24));

        if (Boolean.FALSE.equals(claimed)) {
            // Key exists: return stored response
            String stored = redis.opsForValue().get("idem:" + idempotencyKey);
            System.out.println("Replaying stored result for key: " + idempotencyKey);
            return stored;
        }

        // Process the charge
        String result = "{\"txnId\":\"TXN-001\",\"status\":\"CHARGED\"}";
        redis.opsForValue().set("idem:" + idempotencyKey, result, Duration.ofHours(24));
        System.out.println("Charged " + amount + " to " + accountId);
        return result;
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (first call):
Charged 50000.00 to SAV2001

Expected output (retry):
Replaying stored result for key: IK-20240115-001
```

### When do you use a DB unique constraint?

Use it when the goal is preventing duplicate records and the database can enforce it atomically.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

import org.springframework.dao.DuplicateKeyException;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class LedgerService {

    private final LedgerEntryRepository ledgerRepo;

    public LedgerService(LedgerEntryRepository ledgerRepo) {
        this.ledgerRepo = ledgerRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public String recordEntry(String transactionRef, String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        try {
            // UNIQUE(transaction_reference) in the database
            ledgerRepo.save(new LedgerEntry(transactionRef, accountId, amount));
            System.out.println("Ledger entry recorded: " + transactionRef);
            return "RECORDED";
        } catch (DuplicateKeyException e) {
            System.out.println("Duplicate entry rejected: " + transactionRef);
            return "ALREADY_RECORDED";
        }
    }
}
```

```sql
-- Table with unique constraint
CREATE TABLE ledger_entries (
    id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    transaction_reference VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    account_id VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
    amount DECIMAL(15,2) NOT NULL,
    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT NOW(),
    CONSTRAINT uk_transaction_ref UNIQUE (transaction_reference)
);
```

```text
Expected output (first call):
Ledger entry recorded: TXN-REF-001

Expected output (retry):
Duplicate entry rejected: TXN-REF-001
```

### When do you use state-based validation?

Use it when the operation is a state transition and repeating it is a no-op if the resource is already in the target state.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class AccountClosureService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AccountClosureService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public String closeAccount(String accountId) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();

        if (account.getStatus() == AccountStatus.CLOSED) {
            System.out.println("Account " + accountId + " already closed, skipping");
            return "ALREADY_CLOSED";
        }

        // Conditional update: atomic check-and-act
        int updated = accountRepo.closeIfNotClosed(accountId);
        // UPDATE accounts SET status='CLOSED' WHERE id=? AND status<>'CLOSED'

        if (updated == 0) {
            System.out.println("Account " + accountId + " closed by another thread");
            return "ALREADY_CLOSED";
        }

        System.out.println("Account " + accountId + " closed successfully");
        return "CLOSED";
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (first call):
Account SAV2001 closed successfully

Expected output (second call):
Account SAV2001 already closed, skipping
```

### When do you use a distributed lock?

Use it when a single scarce resource must be handed out exactly once under high concurrency across many instances, and the resource is not a DB row you can constrain.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.UUID;

@Service
public class CouponRedemptionService {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;

    public CouponRedemptionService(StringRedisTemplate redis) {
        this.redis = redis;
    }

    public String redeemCoupon(String couponId, String accountId) {
        String lockKey = "coupon-lock:" + couponId;
        String token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();

        // Acquire lock: SET NX PX ensures only one caller wins
        Boolean acquired = redis.opsForValue()
                .setIfAbsent(lockKey, token, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

        if (Boolean.FALSE.equals(acquired)) {
            System.out.println("Coupon " + couponId + " already claimed by another user");
            return "ALREADY_CLAIMED";
        }

        try {
            // Critical section: redeem the one-time coupon
            System.out.println("Coupon " + couponId + " redeemed by " + accountId);
            return "REDEEMED";
        } finally {
            // Release lock (check token to avoid releasing someone else's lock)
            String current = redis.opsForValue().get(lockKey);
            if (token.equals(current)) {
                redis.delete(lockKey);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (first caller):
Coupon DIWALI50 redeemed by SAV2001

Expected output (second caller, concurrent):
Coupon DIWALI50 already claimed by another user
```

### How do you choose between these techniques?

| Technique | Best For | Strength | Weakness |
|---|---|---|---|
| Idempotency key | Payments, commands | Replays stored response | Needs key store (Redis) |
| Unique constraint | Duplicate record prevention | Database-enforced, survives crashes | Only prevents duplicate rows |
| State-based check | State transitions | No extra storage needed | Needs atomic check-and-act |
| Distributed lock | One-time scarce resource | Works across instances | TTL hazards, latency |

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

public class TechniqueChoice {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Decision guide:");
        System.out.println("  Replaying a response? -> Idempotency key");
        System.out.println("  Preventing duplicate rows? -> Unique constraint");
        System.out.println("  Skipping a completed transition? -> State check");
        System.out.println("  Claiming a non-DB scarce resource? -> Distributed lock");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Preference: DB constraint > state check > idem key > lock");
        System.out.println("Reason: DB guarantees survive crashes; locks have TTL hazards");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Decision guide:
  Replaying a response? -> Idempotency key
  Preventing duplicate rows? -> Unique constraint
  Skipping a completed transition? -> State check
  Claiming a non-DB scarce resource? -> Distributed lock

Preference: DB constraint > state check > idem key > lock
Reason: DB guarantees survive crashes; locks have TTL hazards
```

### How do these techniques compose?

A payment endpoint often uses an idempotency key at the edge AND a unique constraint in the database as a second line of defense.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

public class ComposedIdempotency {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Composed defense for payment:");
        System.out.println("  Layer 1: Idempotency key in Redis (fast, returns stored response)");
        System.out.println("  Layer 2: UNIQUE(transaction_reference) in DB (hard backstop)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("If Redis is down or key expired:");
        System.out.println("  Layer 2 catches the duplicate at the database level");
        System.out.println("  DuplicateKeyException -> return 'already processed'");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Composed defense for payment:
  Layer 1: Idempotency key in Redis (fast, returns stored response)
  Layer 2: UNIQUE(transaction_reference) in DB (hard backstop)

If Redis is down or key expired:
  Layer 2 catches the duplicate at the database level
  DuplicateKeyException -> return 'already processed'
```

### What are the concurrency subtleties?

Two requests with the same idempotency key may arrive simultaneously. The store-and-check must be atomic.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

public class ConcurrencyHandling {
    // WRONG: read-then-write race
    // Thread A: reads key -> not found
    // Thread B: reads key -> not found
    // Thread A: processes + stores
    // Thread B: processes + stores -> DOUBLE CHARGE

    // CORRECT: atomic claim with SET NX or INSERT with unique constraint
    // Thread A: SET NX key -> success (claimed)
    // Thread B: SET NX key -> failure (someone else claimed)
    // Thread B: waits or returns 409

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: read-then-write race -> double charge");
        System.out.println("CORRECT: atomic SET NX or unique INSERT -> single winner");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Handle in-progress case:");
        System.out.println("  If key exists with IN_PROGRESS status:");
        System.out.println("    Option A: wait and poll for result");
        System.out.println("    Option B: return 409 Conflict, client retries later");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: read-then-write race -> double charge
CORRECT: atomic SET NX or unique INSERT -> single winner

Handle in-progress case:
  If key exists with IN_PROGRESS status:
    Option A: wait and poll for result
    Option B: return 409 Conflict, client retries later
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Prefer database-enforced guards over distributed locks wherever possible, because unique constraints survive crashes while locks have TTL and expiry hazards.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  - Unique constraint: cheapest, survives crashes, no extra infra
  - State check: no storage cost, needs atomic conditional update
  - Idempotency key: needed when you must replay the full response
  - Distributed lock: last resort when resource is not a DB row
  - Always handle concurrent same-key: atomic claim, not read-then-write
  - Compose: idempotency key at edge + unique constraint in DB
  - Clean up: TTL on idempotency keys, periodic purge of processed messages
```
